WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.869
-Our next guest
is a supermodel, actress,

00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:04.471
businesswoman,
and now published author.

00:00:04.471 --> 00:00:06.973
Her first book, "My Body,"
is on sale now.

00:00:06.973 --> 00:00:09.442
Please welcome back to the show
Emily Ratajkowski.

00:00:09.442 --> 00:00:11.344
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:17.183 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:17.183 --> 00:00:22.989 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:22.989 --> 00:00:24.557
Welcome back, Emily.
How are you?

00:00:24.557 --> 00:00:26.592
-Oh, I'm good. How are you?
-I'm good.

00:00:26.592 --> 00:00:30.697
It's a trip, but you were here
March 10th two years ago

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:33.032
right before the world
turned upside down.

00:00:33.032 --> 00:00:34.801
-No.
This is, like, the beginning.

00:00:34.801 --> 00:00:38.004
This is the book --
Beginning of the pandemic for me

00:00:38.004 --> 00:00:40.973
was coming here, and then
I went to a Broadway show.

00:00:40.973 --> 00:00:42.875
And I remember being like,
"Is this a big deal?

00:00:42.875 --> 00:00:45.544
Everybody's really crammed
in the bathroom. Whatever."

00:00:45.544 --> 00:00:46.980
You know, all that stuff.
-Yeah.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:48.915
-And then my husband
couldn't sleep that night

00:00:48.915 --> 00:00:50.783
because he was reading stuff
about the pandemic.

00:00:50.783 --> 00:00:52.218
And I rolled over,
and I was like,

00:00:52.218 --> 00:00:53.519
"What's really bothering you?

00:00:53.519 --> 00:00:55.921
Because obviously
something's wrong.

00:00:55.921 --> 00:00:57.423
Because this isn't real."

00:00:57.423 --> 00:00:58.691
I'm like,
"You need to talk about it."

00:00:58.691 --> 00:01:00.192
And then, sure enough,
it was lockdown.

00:01:00.192 --> 00:01:02.028
-It was the pandemic, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:02.028 --> 00:01:03.496
-It was the rare time
where a spouse

00:01:03.496 --> 00:01:05.197
is actually being honest
about what they're thinking.

00:01:05.197 --> 00:01:06.299
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:06.299 --> 00:01:07.633
-I wish --
I really didn't believe him.

00:01:07.633 --> 00:01:08.968
-Yeah.
-He brings it up a lot.

00:01:08.968 --> 00:01:10.870
He's like, "I was right."
-Yeah.

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:13.439
Well, the rare case when you
are, you try to bring it up.

00:01:13.439 --> 00:01:16.109
-Yeah, so it's nice to be back
because Ii feels like the end

00:01:16.109 --> 00:01:18.477
of the pandemic
because I'm back here.

00:01:18.477 --> 00:01:20.080
-I think this is
a nice bookend.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:21.714
-Yeah.
-You also spoke about the fact

00:01:21.714 --> 00:01:24.384
that you were going to write
a book when you were here.

00:01:24.384 --> 00:01:26.518
And I've talked
to some authors who found,

00:01:26.518 --> 00:01:29.288
obviously, the pandemic
was this weird silver lining

00:01:29.288 --> 00:01:30.623
to have time to work on a book.

00:01:30.623 --> 00:01:32.658
And other people
had giant writer's block

00:01:32.658 --> 00:01:34.427
'cause it was
such an anxious time

00:01:34.427 --> 00:01:36.095
and they actually
couldn't be creative.

00:01:36.095 --> 00:01:39.098
How did that time off
from, you know,

00:01:39.098 --> 00:01:42.402
living a normal life work out
for you so far as how you wrote?

00:01:42.402 --> 00:01:46.405
-Well, the other big part
of my last couple year--

00:01:46.405 --> 00:01:48.041
or year, is that I had a baby.

00:01:48.041 --> 00:01:49.676
-Yeah, that changes
a lot of stuff.

00:01:49.676 --> 00:01:51.377
-So that's also crazy.
-Congratulations.

00:01:51.377 --> 00:01:53.546
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Thank you.

00:01:53.546 --> 00:01:57.950
And I think something about
the pandemic, being pregnant,

00:01:57.950 --> 00:02:01.287
having that kind of weird
sense of time that we all had

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:04.958
when we didn't have to show up
places was really good for me.

00:02:04.958 --> 00:02:06.825
But since he has been born,

00:02:06.825 --> 00:02:11.331
I am the most productive person
in the world just because I --

00:02:11.331 --> 00:02:14.400
Every second is for him,
and then there's extra time.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.102
Yeah, so, two weeks postpartum,

00:02:16.102 --> 00:02:19.372
I had to go back to this
'cause I was on deadline.

00:02:19.372 --> 00:02:20.673
So I was, like, writing a book

00:02:20.673 --> 00:02:23.075
and breastfeeding
every two to three hours

00:02:23.075 --> 00:02:26.512
and felt totally insane
and incapable of doing it.

00:02:26.512 --> 00:02:28.448
And now I'm here
talking about it,

00:02:28.448 --> 00:02:30.382
so I think things
have worked out.

00:02:30.382 --> 00:02:32.585
-They are very personal essays,
and I'm wondering --

00:02:32.585 --> 00:02:34.620
Obviously must be cathartic
to both write them,

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:36.723
but now there's this moment
where everybody --

00:02:36.723 --> 00:02:38.591
You're going to share it
with the world.

00:02:38.591 --> 00:02:40.760
Like, how are you feeling
in this time

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:43.362
where people are going to
crack it open for the first?

00:02:43.362 --> 00:02:45.631
-Yeah, I mean,
I think that writing it

00:02:45.631 --> 00:02:47.900
was sort of an act of control
for me,

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:51.938
and creating something that
was just mine felt so good.

00:02:51.938 --> 00:02:54.707
And, you know, I think
now it's a little bit of, like,

00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:57.810
letting it go into the world
and letting go of that control.

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:01.681
And it's been really weird to be
talking about the book so much

00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:03.716
when it's not been
available to people.

00:03:03.716 --> 00:03:05.284
-Yeah.
-You know? Like, I hate it.

00:03:05.284 --> 00:03:07.653
Because I'm like,
"Well, just read the book.

00:03:07.653 --> 00:03:09.822
I don't want to talk
about it anymore."

00:03:09.822 --> 00:03:11.824
And I feel kind of bad almost.
-Yeah.

00:03:11.824 --> 00:03:13.926
-You don't want to hear
about it anymore.

00:03:13.926 --> 00:03:15.494
So, yeah, now it's nice

00:03:15.494 --> 00:03:17.597
that people can actually
go and see for themselves.

00:03:17.597 --> 00:03:20.566
Even the essays I was talking
about a year and a half ago,

00:03:20.566 --> 00:03:21.900
I'm ready for people
to read them.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:23.803
-You -- Did you share it,

00:03:23.803 --> 00:03:26.972
along the way, drafts with
parents, friends, family?

00:03:26.972 --> 00:03:29.641
-Yeah, I did with friends.
I would, you know --

00:03:29.641 --> 00:03:31.543
Writing's such a lonely process

00:03:31.543 --> 00:03:34.580
and especially in the pandemic
and even before that, you know?

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:36.616
You'd have a bad day,
and it would be just like --

00:03:36.616 --> 00:03:38.785
You have to sit with the fact
that you had a bad day.

00:03:38.785 --> 00:03:41.888
But when you had a good day, you
want to share it with someone.

00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:43.923
So I'd send my friends a text
and be like,

00:03:43.923 --> 00:03:46.159
"No pressure.
Here's 10,000 words.

00:03:46.159 --> 00:03:48.227
Don't worry about it.
Nobody else has read it.

00:03:48.227 --> 00:03:50.596
And if you hate it, I'm going
to want to kill myself."

00:03:50.596 --> 00:03:54.967
But I did have
a lot of female friends,

00:03:54.967 --> 00:03:57.837
those early readers --
and my husband, as well.

00:03:57.837 --> 00:03:59.105
And that was -- It was nice.

00:03:59.105 --> 00:04:01.907
It was just like getting
a credit for turning it in.

00:04:01.907 --> 00:04:07.146
You know? Just doing the work.
I needed that motivation, so...

00:04:07.146 --> 00:04:09.081
-My mom was
a middle-school teacher,

00:04:09.081 --> 00:04:10.282
and she taught me French.

00:04:10.282 --> 00:04:11.751
Your dad was
a high-school teacher?

00:04:11.751 --> 00:04:13.319
-Yes, he was.
-And he was your teacher?

00:04:13.319 --> 00:04:15.455
-Yes.
He was my painting teacher.

00:04:15.455 --> 00:04:18.790
He was really cool.
His nickname was "Radda."

00:04:18.790 --> 00:04:20.392
-Wow. Oh.
That's a really good --

00:04:20.392 --> 00:04:21.527
So, like, Mr. Radda?

00:04:21.527 --> 00:04:23.562
-No, just Radda.
-Wow.

00:04:23.562 --> 00:04:26.299
He was a teacher
who was, like, "Lose the --

00:04:26.299 --> 00:04:27.633
Leave the 'Mister' at the door."

00:04:27.633 --> 00:04:30.202
-Yes. It was so --
He was, like, that guy.

00:04:30.202 --> 00:04:32.271
He would take roll
at the end of class.

00:04:32.271 --> 00:04:33.406
-At the end?

00:04:33.406 --> 00:04:35.107
-Uh-huh. So people
could show up late.

00:04:35.107 --> 00:04:38.110
And, like,
the surf guys loved him.

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:39.879
He wore flip-flops.
I grew up in San Diego.

00:04:39.879 --> 00:04:41.247
It's very specific.

00:04:41.247 --> 00:04:42.981
Like, my high school
was outdoors.

00:04:42.981 --> 00:04:44.216
You know what I mean?

00:04:44.216 --> 00:04:45.784
I'm very opposite
of the East Coast vibe.

00:04:45.784 --> 00:04:47.519
But, yeah, no,
he was really cool.

00:04:47.519 --> 00:04:48.854
So it worked in my favor.

00:04:48.854 --> 00:04:50.356
But -- How about your mom?

00:04:50.356 --> 00:04:53.192
-My mom was also very cool.

00:04:53.192 --> 00:04:55.361
She was.
-Okay.

00:04:55.361 --> 00:04:57.830
-She was beloved.
Like, she was a beloved teacher.

00:04:57.830 --> 00:04:59.098
-Mrs. Meyers.
-Madame.

00:04:59.098 --> 00:05:00.466
Please. "Missus." Come on.

00:05:00.466 --> 00:05:02.101
-Oh, my God.
-Madame, yeah.

00:05:02.101 --> 00:05:05.104
I've said this before,
but the craziest thing

00:05:05.104 --> 00:05:07.173
about going back to my hometown
is she was so beloved

00:05:07.173 --> 00:05:09.609
that I'll be in a grocery store,
and someone will come up

00:05:09.609 --> 00:05:11.276
the way I will clock it as,

00:05:11.276 --> 00:05:14.279
"Oh, here we go. They recognize
a local celebrity."

00:05:14.279 --> 00:05:15.781
And they'll say like,

00:05:15.781 --> 00:05:17.583
"Will you please say
hello to Madame for me?"

00:05:17.583 --> 00:05:19.619
And I'm like, "Oh, they
don't care about me at all."

00:05:19.619 --> 00:05:22.722
-So, I have the same thing
with my dad. It's really funny.

00:05:22.722 --> 00:05:25.791
People will, you know, reach out
or come up to me and be like,

00:05:25.791 --> 00:05:27.326
"I had your dad."

00:05:27.326 --> 00:05:29.862
He's retired now, but
they'll say, like, 20 years ago,

00:05:29.862 --> 00:05:31.730
whatever,
and it kind of blows my mind.

00:05:31.730 --> 00:05:33.432
-You get to be a parent now.
-Yeah.

00:05:33.432 --> 00:05:35.401
-You have an 8-month-old.
-Yeah.

00:05:35.401 --> 00:05:38.938
-A little too young to enjoy
Halloween or was he into it?

00:05:38.938 --> 00:05:40.173
-I -- Well, okay.

00:05:40.173 --> 00:05:41.841
So, I think he actually
really enjoyed it.

00:05:41.841 --> 00:05:44.510
He's 8 months old today.
It's his birthday.

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:46.511
-[ Cheers and applause ]
-Very exciting.

00:05:46.511 --> 00:05:49.181
Thank you. I'll tell him.

00:05:49.181 --> 00:05:51.250
And we dressed him as a bear,

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:52.851
because my husband's
last name is Bear,

00:05:52.851 --> 00:05:54.520
so it felt necessary.

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:56.989
But we took him out.
We went to a music --

00:05:56.989 --> 00:06:00.526
a baby music class
where everybody was dressed up.

00:06:00.526 --> 00:06:01.994
And we thought he was
gonna fall asleep,

00:06:01.994 --> 00:06:04.530
and we were walking out on the
streets, and he was partying.

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:06.431
He loved it.
We live in Tribeca,

00:06:06.431 --> 00:06:08.768
so there were so many kids
on the streets dressed up,

00:06:08.768 --> 00:06:10.970
and he was just
looking around, loving it.

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:12.405
It's amazing.
-It's really the best.

00:06:12.405 --> 00:06:13.706
-Yeah, did your kids --

00:06:13.706 --> 00:06:17.943
-Yeah, I mean, they're 5 1/2
and 3 1/2, so this is --

00:06:17.943 --> 00:06:20.279
this was the first year
where they very strongly knew

00:06:20.279 --> 00:06:23.448
what they wanted to be,
which was a --

00:06:23.448 --> 00:06:24.884
the 3 1/2-year-old was a pirate

00:06:24.884 --> 00:06:28.120
and the 5 1/2-year-old
was a cruise-ship captain.

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:29.521
[ Laughter ]
-Whoa.

00:06:29.521 --> 00:06:33.192
-Yeah.
-Not like the COVID cruise ship.

00:06:33.192 --> 00:06:34.326
-Maybe.
-Since we're talking about --

00:06:34.326 --> 00:06:35.828
Okay, all right.
-Yeah, maybe that was

00:06:35.828 --> 00:06:37.463
what he was.
Because he was like,

00:06:37.463 --> 00:06:41.067
"Nothing's scarier than being
inside a boat with COVID."

00:06:41.067 --> 00:06:42.635
[ Laughter ]
-That documentary

00:06:42.635 --> 00:06:44.270
really blew my mind.
-Yeah, exactly.

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:45.771
-Yeah, okay, cool.

00:06:45.771 --> 00:06:48.674
-He was like, "Before corona,
the norovirus, like, you wished

00:06:48.674 --> 00:06:50.843
you got corona instead of that."

00:06:50.843 --> 00:06:53.179
So, I have a question about
your professional career.

00:06:53.179 --> 00:06:54.647
-Uh-huh.
Yes, yes.

00:06:54.647 --> 00:06:56.415
-Do you have any memory
of this photo?

00:06:56.415 --> 00:06:58.417
[ Laughter ]
-I do. I do.

00:06:58.417 --> 00:07:00.085
-You do.
-Yes, I do.

00:07:00.085 --> 00:07:01.821
-Pete was being
pretty hard on himself.

00:07:01.821 --> 00:07:03.689
-I know.
I saw that.

00:07:03.689 --> 00:07:05.190
He was being
pretty hard on himself.

00:07:05.190 --> 00:07:06.325
I think he's great.

00:07:06.325 --> 00:07:07.659
-On the day,
did he come off as a pro?

00:07:07.659 --> 00:07:10.363
-He totally --
he's a professional.

00:07:10.363 --> 00:07:11.630
First of all, you should
know that about Pete.

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:13.766
-Yeah.
-And he got into character

00:07:13.766 --> 00:07:16.502
in a real way and was kind of
doing, like, a...

00:07:16.502 --> 00:07:20.639
[ Laughter ]
That worked really well.

00:07:20.639 --> 00:07:21.841
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:07:21.841 --> 00:07:24.276
-He was a fish
at the end of the day.

00:07:24.276 --> 00:07:26.779
-As a model, you know,
I was like, "Wow!"

00:07:26.779 --> 00:07:29.415
-As a model, be honest, when
you show up and there's someone

00:07:29.415 --> 00:07:31.650
with maybe a little bit less
of a pedigree, you know,

00:07:31.650 --> 00:07:35.020
the guy who's there to be the
not a model in the model shoot,

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:36.955
for a minute, are you like,
"All right.

00:07:36.955 --> 00:07:38.724
Let's see what you got,
Davidson"?

00:07:38.724 --> 00:07:40.792
-I think you're being
a little mean.

00:07:40.792 --> 00:07:42.528
-I'm only asking.
-Pete's -- he's got the height.

00:07:42.528 --> 00:07:44.296
-He is a tall guy.
-Obviously, women find him

00:07:44.296 --> 00:07:46.665
very attractive.
-Yeah, well obviously.

00:07:46.665 --> 00:07:49.335
Did you hear he's got a show
on Tubi right now?

00:07:49.335 --> 00:07:51.937
-That I did. I did.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:51.937 --> 00:07:54.140
-I mean they don't just
give that to everybody.

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:56.875
-No, they don't.
Very special, very exclusive.

00:07:56.875 --> 00:07:58.243
[ Laughter ] Yes.
-Yeah, you're right.

00:07:58.243 --> 00:07:59.878
I don't know why I'm like,
"How is that guy a model?"

00:07:59.878 --> 00:08:02.648
-Yeah, yeah, I feel like only
other men feel that way.

00:08:02.648 --> 00:08:04.383
Guys are like,
"Wow. What's that guy got?"

00:08:04.383 --> 00:08:06.619
And I'm like, I mean,
he seems super charming.

00:08:06.619 --> 00:08:07.820
-Yeah.
-He's vulnerable.

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:08.920
-Yeah.
-He's lovely.

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:10.456
-Great.
-His fingernail polish

00:08:10.456 --> 00:08:11.924
is awesome.
He looks good.

00:08:11.924 --> 00:08:13.325
-There you go.
-He's great.

00:08:13.325 --> 00:08:15.594
-All right. Well, I'm sold.
I'm gonna have him back on.

00:08:15.594 --> 00:08:17.062
-Good relationship
with his mother.

00:08:17.062 --> 00:08:18.264
-Yeah, great relationship
with his mother.

00:08:18.264 --> 00:08:19.631
-We love it.
-He's just a sweetheart.

00:08:19.631 --> 00:08:22.200
-It's hard to find them.
-Yeah, there you go.

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:23.869
Congratulations again.

00:08:23.869 --> 00:08:25.437
This must be the best feeling
to finish a book.

00:08:25.437 --> 00:08:29.141
I think that that's --
having only read one --

00:08:29.141 --> 00:08:30.542
[ Laughter ]
More than one.

00:08:30.542 --> 00:08:31.844
-More than one.

00:08:31.844 --> 00:08:33.178
-I still can't believe
that people write a book,

00:08:33.178 --> 00:08:36.582
so you've done the impossible.
-Thank you, and that's

00:08:36.582 --> 00:08:38.183
why I just didn't tell myself

00:08:38.183 --> 00:08:39.885
I was writing a book
until it was done.

00:08:39.885 --> 00:08:42.188
Because it just felt
like the impossible.

00:08:42.188 --> 00:08:43.522
-I want to see it.
Oh, very good.

00:08:43.522 --> 00:08:45.157
I was gonna -- my last thing
was gonna be

00:08:45.157 --> 00:08:46.325
your book-jacket photo.

00:08:46.325 --> 00:08:47.860
How much time
did you spend on this?

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:51.596
-So, actually, my best friend
took that with a digital camera,

00:08:51.596 --> 00:08:53.933
and she's very excited
because she got photo credit.

00:08:53.933 --> 00:08:55.067
-Oh, that's a big deal.

00:08:55.067 --> 00:08:56.468
-So forever she'll have
a little name.

00:08:56.468 --> 00:08:57.737
You know, it's good.
-Yeah, that's very nice.

00:08:57.737 --> 00:08:59.438
-It made her happy.
-You paid it forward.

00:08:59.438 --> 00:09:00.606
-Yeah.
-Hey, thanks for being here.

00:09:00.606 --> 00:09:01.940
Congratulations on the baby.

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:03.409
Congratulations on the book.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:03.409 --> 00:09:05.811
Emily Ratajkowski, everybody.
"My Body" is on sale right now

00:09:05.811 --> 00:09:07.913
wherever books are sold.
But please remember

00:09:07.913 --> 00:09:10.182
to support your local
and independent bookstores.

